gan a career as an attorney  [Music]  I think some of my early musical  training was singing in the car and then  when I got older I was finding myself  singing Prince and Michael Jackson songs  and realizing that everybody can't do  that and so I the more I did it the more  people wanted me to do it more and uh  and so singing fall said it was just  been something that I've been I get to  do in my my job when I walked into  rehearsal today I noticed people of all  ages men women people of all races  there's such a great camaraderie in this  group well music is a universal language  and it connects us all this is the  reason why we're here and it's the tool  that we use to connect the Navy to the  people  musician first class David Smith plays  trumpet in the band's brass section the  band's 170 members and it's 11 ensembles  perform more than 1300 shows per year  all around the globe  [Music]  it was my first year we learned a  japanese piece we had selected a song  that was written in honor of those that  they lost in the giant tsunami in 2011  that you happenes people there they  started weeping and they came up to us  and they shake our hands afterwards  through broken English they're they're  thanking us for honoring them honoring  their people honoring their country we  shared an experience established good  faith and we just we created a  relationship and it was like I can't  imagine anything more impactful  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  and these servicemen are not typically  subject to combat deployment the way  other sailors in the Navy are and it's  so interesting because first they  audition for the band and then if they  make it they join the Navy because I was  wondering about what happens if you  don't make it I joke with Chelsea there  are clothes your way but as you've just  heard you can understand why someone  would want this opportunity and also why  thousands of tickets to their Sunday  show sold out in about seven minutes Wow  great piece  we d